************************************
*   Compilation And Installation   *
************************************

First install build dependencies. In Debian-based systems, they are:

 * g++ >= 12
 * qt6-base-dev (for Qt6)
 * qt6-svg-dev (for hard-coded SVG icons)
 * qt6-base-dev-tools (for localization -- optional)

In Arch-based systems, the required package are:

 * gcc (or gcc-multilib for multilib systems)
 * qt6-base (for Qt6)
 * qt6-svg (for hard-coded SVG icons)
 * qt6-tools (for localization -- optional)

In Red Hat based systems like Fedora:

 * gcc-c++
 * qt6-qtbase-devel
 * qt6-qtsvg-devel
 * qt6-qttools-devel

And, finally, in OpenSUSE:

 * gcc-c++
 * libqt6-qtbase-devel
 * libqt6-qtsvg-devel
 * libqt6-qttools-devel

In addition, you need to have libarchive (bsdtar), gzip and 7z installed because Arqiver is a front-end for them, but they are not build dependencies.

With cmake
==========
Then, open a terminal inside this folder and issue the following commands:

	mkdir build && cd build
	cmake ..
	make
	sudo make install

In some distros, you may need to be more explicit and use `cmake .. -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr` for installing Arqiver under `/usr`, in contrast to `/usr/local`. The latter place may not be good in Linux distros and could cause troubles later.

With qmake
==========
If you prefer qmake, use these commands instead:

	qmake && make
	sudo make install

Afterward, you could use this command to clean the source directory:

	make distclean

**********************************
*   Translation (Localization)   *
**********************************

The file 'data/translations/arqiver.ts' can serve as the basis for translation. The translated file should be saved in the same directory as "arqiver_LN.ts", where "LN" is the abbreviation for the target language, like "de", "fr", etc.

If you have translated Arqiver's GUI into your language, please make a "Pull Request" (PR) at https://github.com/tsujan/Arqiver for your work to be merged into Arqiver!

*******************
*   macOS Build   *
*******************

Use a Qt 6.6 or newer installation for your target architecture and macOS
version. Both CMake and qmake create arqiver.app with its icon, archive file
associations and translations. The default installation prefix is /Applications.

For example, with Homebrew and Apple's Command Line Tools installed:

    brew install qt cmake ninja sevenzip libarchive
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build
    cmake --install build

To install elsewhere, set CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX when configuring. Alternatively:

    mkdir build && cd build
    qmake ../arqiver.pro
    make
    make install

If qmake is not on PATH, use the qmake executable from your Qt installation.
For a custom qmake installation directory, pass PREFIX=/your/directory.

Arqiver uses macOS's built-in gzip. It prefers a bundled bsdtar or Homebrew's
libarchive and falls back to macOS's built-in libarchive-based tar. Homebrew's
libarchive supports additional compression formats such as Zstandard and LZ4.
Its Preferences allow selecting another bsdtar executable. Do not select GNU tar.
For 7z-based formats, install sevenzip (7zz) or p7zip (7z). Arqiver also searches
/opt/homebrew/bin and /usr/local/bin when launched from Finder.

For distribution to a machine without Qt, run your Qt installation's
macdeployqt on the completed app bundle before copying it. For Homebrew Qt:

    macdeployqt build/arqiver.app -libpath="$(brew --prefix)/lib"

A standalone 7zz (or 7z) executable can optionally be included in
arqiver.app/Contents/MacOS; it takes priority over installed executables.
Include the helper's required libraries and license notices when distributing
it. Sign the final bundle after deploying Qt and any helpers. Signing identity
and notarization are handled by the distributor.

Finder's Open With menu and dropping archives onto the Dock icon open archive
windows. Additional documents open in separate windows, so opening one archive
does not discard edits or interrupt a process in another window.
